# Patch:        040-redirect-avx512-intrinsics
# Phase:        pre
# Target:       baby-bear/src/x86_64_avx512/packing.rs, baby-bear/src/x86_64_avx512/poseidon2.rs, field/src/packed/interleaves.rs, field/src/packed/x86_64_avx.rs, monty-31/src/x86_64_avx512/packing.rs, monty-31/src/x86_64_avx512/poseidon1.rs, monty-31/src/x86_64_avx512/poseidon2.rs, monty-31/src/x86_64_avx512/utils.rs
# Hunks:        16
# Class:        cfg-hide
# Gap:          docs/extractor-issues.md 210: the `core::arch` intrinsics have no
#               Rust bodies to extract; and 213: aeneas rejects `asm!`.
# Cost:         none in a normal build: every change is behind
#               cfg(hax_backend_lean). In the extracted AVX-512 variants each
#               intrinsic is its model in `arch/` (p3-arch-shims), trusted:
#               reviewed against Intel's pseudocode, not yet tested against the
#               hardware (`arch/tests/avx512.rs` needs an AVX-512 CPU). The two
#               `confuse_compiler` barriers are the identity, and the two masked
#               `vmovshdup` `asm!` blocks are the intrinsics for that
#               instruction: stand-ins, listed in TRUST.md.
# Upstreamable: no: the models exist only for the extractor. Models of the
#               intrinsics in the extractor's library, and support for `asm!`,
#               would make it unnecessary.
# Drop when:    the extractor models the `core::arch::x86_64` AVX-512 intrinsics
#               and `asm!`.
# Test:         xcheck p3-baby-bear x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -Ctarget-feature=+avx512f;
#               xcheck p3-baby-bear x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -Ctarget-feature=+avx512f --cfg hax_backend_lean
# Mutation:     monty-31's packing imports the models in a normal build, which
#               has no p3-arch-shims dependency, so the AVX-512 build fails.
#
# As 020 does for AVX2: every file on BabyBear's AVX-512 path that names a
# vector or mask type, or calls an intrinsic, imports `core::arch::x86_64` in a
# normal build and `p3_arch_shims::x86_64` under the cfg. The models have the
# intrinsics' names and signatures, over `repr(transparent)` lane arrays (the
# masks are `u16` and `u8`, as in `core::arch`), so no other line changes. 020
# already makes p3-arch-shims a dependency of field, monty-31 and baby-bear
# under the cfg, and redirects the AVX2 imports that the AVX-512 path shares
# (`x86_64_avx.rs`'s `__m256i`, `__m128i` and intrinsics).
#
# monty-31's AVX-512 packing has four `asm!` blocks, which aeneas rejects:
#
# - `confuse_compiler` and `confuse_compiler_256` are `asm!("/*{0}*/",
#   inlateout(zmm_reg) x => y, ...)` (and `ymm_reg`), followed by a check that
#   `y == x`: the value is `x`, and the purpose is to stop LLVM from rewriting
#   the multiplications that follow. Under the cfg each is the identity.
# - `mask_movehdup_epi32` and `mask_movehdup_epi32_256` are the single
#   instruction `vmovshdup {src_dst}{{{k}}}, {a}`, on zmm and ymm registers:
#   `vmovshdup` with merge masking into `src`, the destination's input. That is
#   the instruction of `_mm512_mask_movehdup_ps(src, k, a)` and
#   `_mm256_mask_movehdup_ps(src, k, a)`, on the vectors cast to and from
#   floats (`_mm512_castsi512_ps`, `_mm512_castps_si512`, and the 256-bit
#   casts), which change no bits. The code's comment says it uses `asm!` only to
#   keep LLVM from compiling the intrinsic to a slower `vpermt2ps`. Under the
#   cfg each is that intrinsic call.
#
# `asm` and `unreachable_unchecked`, which only the blocks use, are imported
# only in a normal build. The models say nothing about the optimisations; the
# four stand-ins are listed in TRUST.md.
#
# The tests compile the AVX-512 path for x86_64: as shipped, which can only use
# `core::arch` since p3-arch-shims is not a dependency without the cfg; and
# under the cfg, which shows that the models fit every call site.
#
# Tested:     test-pre-patches.py: 6715 of 6769 upstream tests pass as
#             shipped, 6715 with the pre-patches; 0 divergences from this patch.
#
